This still needs to be moved into the token, e.g. like we do for `user` on 148. That's the only way it's guaranteed to be signed. We should also likely just pass the scopes, maybe without launch, e.g.,

```java
externalToken.setNote("launchTypes", getLaunchScopes());

//...

private String getLaunchScopes(String scope) {
	Arrays.stream("launch/patient launch/encounter".split("\\s")).filter(it -> it.startsWith("launch/")).map(it -> it.substring(6)).collect(
			Collectors.joining(" "));
}
```

Then we do need to handle the difference in OpenMRS, but at least this way we're less directly tied to knowing that, e.g., we only care about one or the other scope.

This one needs fixing: `client_request_param_resource` is never set, so the `resource` alias can't fire. `resource` is a known parameter to Keycloak (`AuthzEndpointRequestParser.KNOWN_REQ_PARAMS` adds `OIDCLoginProtocol.RESOURCE_PARAM`, which is `"resource"`), and `[word removed]Endpoint` only writes the `client_request_param_` prefix for parameters it *doesn't* recognise (line 393). Recognised ones go in as plain client notes under their own name, via `performActionOnParameters` at line 350. So a client that sends `resource` and no `aud` gets rejected with "SMART App Launch requires an 'aud' parameter naming the FHIR server", even though the comment above says the alias exists because real clients send it. `audience` is fine, that one genuinely is unknown to Keycloak.

`authenticate_shouldAcceptTheResourceAndAudienceAliases` passes because the test's `present()` helper writes the prefixed note itself, so it can't see this.

Keycloak reads these both ways in its own code: `AbstractOAuth2IdentityProvider.appendForwardedParameters` tries the prefixed note and falls back to the plain name. Same shape here:

```suggestion
		for (String param : AUDIENCE_PARAMS) {
			String value = authSession.getClientNote(CLIENT_REQUEST_PARAM_PREFIX + param);
			if (StringUtils.isBlank(value)) {
				value = authSession.getClientNote(param);
			}
			if (StringUtils.isNotBlank(value)) {
				return value;
			}
		}
```

Worth adding a case that presents `resource` under the plain note, otherwise the same gap reopens. If you'd rather not carry the alias at all, dropping `resource` from `AUDIENCE_PARAMS` and from the parameterised test is the other consistent option; what shouldn't stay is a documented alias that does nothing.
